WEBVTT

1
00:48:25.640 --> 00:48:27.680
Hello.

2
00:48:27.680 --> 00:48:30.680
Good afternoon, good evening,
depending on where you're hearing from.

3
00:48:32.600 --> 00:48:36.400
I'm Faith Samuel Bassey.

4
00:48:36.400 --> 00:48:40.920
[?] And I'm here to do my presentation

5
00:48:40.920 --> 00:48:44.760
in collaboration with my super mentor.

6
00:48:45.800 --> 00:48:47.560
And I want to share my slides.

7
00:48:47.560 --> 00:48:50.560
Hope you don't mind.

8
00:48:50.640 --> 00:48:51.760
I’m talking on “Reacting

9
00:48:51.760 --> 00:48:55.560
to Electronic Literature
in Akwa Ibom State, Nigeria:

10
00:48:55.560 --> 00:48:59.920
The Challenges, Possibilities,
and the Call for Accessibility.”

11
00:49:01.200 --> 00:49:03.600
In my second slide here,

12
00:49:03.600 --> 00:49:06.360
I have abstracted the key points.

13
00:49:06.360 --> 00:49:10.760
Electronic literature
remains an emerging field in Nigeria,

14
00:49:11.360 --> 00:49:14.360
and we have challenges

15
00:49:15.640 --> 00:49:18.000
which range from technological,

16
00:49:18.000 --> 00:49:20.960
educational, and institutional.

17
00:49:20.960 --> 00:49:23.960
And we're going to reflect on
representing the Water Seller, “Mai Ruwa,”

18
00:49:25.240 --> 00:49:28.240
and the focus on accessibility
and the audience engagement,

19
00:49:28.240 --> 00:49:31.240
both for mobile-friendly and inclusive

20
00:49:31.240 --> 00:49:34.240
electronic literature.

21
00:49:35.080 --> 00:49:38.200
And in the next slide here,

22
00:49:39.160 --> 00:49:39.840
we have the

23
00:49:39.840 --> 00:49:43.000
introduction of digital literature.

24
00:49:43.000 --> 00:49:46.000
This has transformed the way our stories

25
00:49:46.000 --> 00:49:49.680
can be created,
distributed, and experienced.

26
00:49:49.960 --> 00:49:53.480
Unlike a traditional print,
electronic literature

27
00:49:53.960 --> 00:49:57.200
depends on digital technologies

28
00:49:57.200 --> 00:50:00.440
for its production and reception,

29
00:50:00.880 --> 00:50:03.520
and often, as we all know, incorporates

30
00:50:03.520 --> 00:50:07.920
hyperlinks, multimedia elements,
interactive structures,

31
00:50:07.920 --> 00:50:12.080
and non-linear narratives,
and invites readers to participate

32
00:50:12.120 --> 00:50:15.120
actively.

33
00:50:15.800 --> 00:50:17.720
Now we have

34
00:50:17.720 --> 00:50:20.120
the current situation.

35
00:50:20.120 --> 00:50:23.120
We have limited awareness.

36
00:50:23.280 --> 00:50:27.160
The emergence of e-literature
in Nigeria presents both exciting

37
00:50:27.200 --> 00:50:31.040
opportunities and significant challenges.

38
00:50:31.280 --> 00:50:35.440
On one hand,
the widespread adoption of smartphones

39
00:50:35.440 --> 00:50:39.560
and digital technologies
offers unprecedented possibilities

40
00:50:39.560 --> 00:50:43.800
for digital innovation
and audience engagement.

41
00:50:44.360 --> 00:50:47.360
On the other hand, inadequate

42
00:50:48.760 --> 00:50:52.120
technologies
and limited scholarly expertise

43
00:50:52.440 --> 00:50:58.280
and lack of institutional support hinder
the growth of electronic literature here

44
00:50:58.280 --> 00:51:01.280
in Nigeria.

45
00:51:04.160 --> 00:51:06.760
Now go to slide

46
00:51:08.680 --> 00:51:11.680
four.

47
00:51:11.920 --> 00:51:12.760
We have

48
00:51:12.760 --> 00:51:15.240
limited awareness outside
specialized circles.

49
00:51:15.240 --> 00:51:19.600
Few scholars and practitioners,
you know, we have limited people

50
00:51:19.640 --> 00:51:22.640
who are trained in this field.

51
00:51:22.840 --> 00:51:25.840
And the next slide is going to show

52
00:51:25.880 --> 00:51:30.440
the opportunities in the Nigerian context,
the positive developments.

53
00:51:30.640 --> 00:51:33.640
What we have here majorly: we have

54
00:51:33.960 --> 00:51:38.400
people having access to smartphones,
and the growing

55
00:51:38.720 --> 00:51:42.760
digital literacy, strong
social media engagement among the youth.

56
00:51:43.800 --> 00:51:48.120
So what we have to do
is the expansion of mobile

57
00:51:50.040 --> 00:51:52.080
literature,
just like the one I'm going to have

58
00:51:52.080 --> 00:51:55.080
in my next slide.

59
00:51:55.080 --> 00:51:58.040
This is a project in collaboration

60
00:51:58.040 --> 00:52:02.960
with my mentor Deena,
the Water Seller, otherwise called “Mai

61
00:52:02.960 --> 00:52:08.000
Ruwa.” It's one of the first digital-born
African literature works.

62
00:52:08.240 --> 00:52:12.560
And from the next slide,
you see the woman with the baby

63
00:52:12.560 --> 00:52:16.520
on her back and the water pot
she carries on the head.

64
00:52:17.200 --> 00:52:19.440
We have all those notes there.

65
00:52:19.440 --> 00:52:23.960
And we have the storyline: the water
she carries, the questions she asks,

66
00:52:24.000 --> 00:52:29.880
the body she cherishes, the voice
she uses, the pain she knows, the strength

67
00:52:29.880 --> 00:52:34.720
she understands, the love
she shares, and the future she hopes for.

68
00:52:35.280 --> 00:52:39.440
This work can be read
through the mobile phone.

69
00:52:40.440 --> 00:52:44.440
And it's also online, published

70
00:52:45.840 --> 00:52:47.640
in the New River Journal,

71
00:52:47.640 --> 00:52:51.480
and you can also find it on
Deena Larsen’s website.

72
00:52:52.360 --> 00:52:55.840
And this work can be read
through the non-linear work

73
00:52:55.880 --> 00:52:58.880
anyhow, just by clicking the notes.

74
00:52:58.880 --> 00:53:02.440
And it has different interpretations.

75
00:53:02.440 --> 00:53:06.400
The reader is also a creator of meaning.

76
00:53:09.440 --> 00:53:12.560
The Water Seller challenges traditional
publishing models, enables global

77
00:53:12.560 --> 00:53:15.880
readership, and demonstrates

78
00:53:15.880 --> 00:53:18.880
possibilities
for African electronic literature.

79
00:53:18.920 --> 00:53:21.920
We can find it on the website down below.

80
00:53:23.800 --> 00:53:26.080
Now, I want to talk on my experience

81
00:53:26.080 --> 00:53:29.880
in presenting electronic literature,
the Mai Ruwa, at

82
00:53:31.000 --> 00:53:34.000
the University French Teachers’
Association of Nigeria, UFTAN,

83
00:53:34.600 --> 00:53:37.720
at the Nnamdi Azikiwe University
last year.

84
00:53:38.640 --> 00:53:41.720
It was a live demonstration
of electronic literature work.

85
00:53:41.920 --> 00:53:43.920
That was what it was supposed to be.

86
00:53:43.920 --> 00:53:46.040
But there was so much challenge.

87
00:53:46.040 --> 00:53:47.200
We have technological

88
00:53:47.200 --> 00:53:51.400
challenges, the problems
we encountered: projector malfunctioning,

89
00:53:51.480 --> 00:53:55.360
no internet connection, inability
to demonstrate the work directly.

90
00:53:55.680 --> 00:54:00.280
Now, the assumption
about the technological

91
00:54:00.280 --> 00:54:04.000
infrastructure fails
in many academic environments.

92
00:54:04.680 --> 00:54:07.400
We had audience reaction.

93
00:54:07.400 --> 00:54:09.320
They were so excited.

94
00:54:09.320 --> 00:54:11.360
They were enthusiastic to learn.

95
00:54:11.360 --> 00:54:15.480
But there were also other concerns.

96
00:54:15.480 --> 00:54:18.880
Now, the question that was raised
was that, you know,

97
00:54:18.920 --> 00:54:24.240
in a traditional setting, in literature,
African and traditional literature,

98
00:54:24.440 --> 00:54:28.360
when you want to do the analysis
of a particular text or novel,

99
00:54:28.360 --> 00:54:32.120
so to say,
you must have a theory to back it up with.

100
00:54:32.320 --> 00:54:35.240
So the question that was raised was:

101
00:54:35.240 --> 00:54:39.280
what are the theories that we can use to

102
00:54:40.800 --> 00:54:43.800
analyze electronic literature?

103
00:54:45.320 --> 00:54:48.320
And—

104
00:54:49.280 --> 00:54:52.120
So, I was able to tell them

105
00:54:52.120 --> 00:54:56.760
that depending on the story
you have to tell, depending on the story

106
00:54:56.760 --> 00:55:00.560
in your project, you can use

107
00:55:00.560 --> 00:55:04.920
any kind of theory that suits it.

108
00:55:05.040 --> 00:55:10.800
I brought out a personal theory
that they could use in that my work,

109
00:55:10.840 --> 00:55:15.440
because my work talks about the woman
and the tradition that impeded her life,

110
00:55:15.440 --> 00:55:18.920
and how she’s supposed to conquer
despite the tradition.

111
00:55:19.240 --> 00:55:24.040
And to them,
the woman was supposed to go to school;

112
00:55:24.040 --> 00:55:27.520
in the picture,
she was able to conquer this challenge.

113
00:55:27.640 --> 00:55:29.720
That was also a major problem.

114
00:55:29.720 --> 00:55:30.800
Then what we have

115
00:55:30.800 --> 00:55:34.640
now is that the challenges are:
we have limited readership, because

116
00:55:35.280 --> 00:55:39.640
back here, you find out
that people are not really funded;

117
00:55:39.680 --> 00:55:44.000
minimal academic
attention; difficulty in measuring impact.

118
00:55:44.000 --> 00:55:46.480
That’s a lot of invisibility.

119
00:55:46.480 --> 00:55:49.600
So you realize that,
because of funding— I’ve heard

120
00:55:49.880 --> 00:55:53.360
there is someone that’s supposed to be
presenting today, you have to go somewhere

121
00:55:53.360 --> 00:55:57.600
to do a recording file
because of network challenges.

122
00:55:57.800 --> 00:56:00.040
So that's the issue we have here.

123
00:56:00.040 --> 00:56:03.040
So it was also of the opinion that

124
00:56:03.360 --> 00:56:08.280
if we can do a call
to action, that we have more

125
00:56:09.840 --> 00:56:10.600
teaching, more

126
00:56:10.600 --> 00:56:13.960
training on the community
people for us over here.

127
00:56:13.960 --> 00:56:18.840
And, being that the infrastructure
remains a challenge.

128
00:56:20.040 --> 00:56:21.800
There was a piece on access today.

129
00:56:21.800 --> 00:56:24.880
The question was, what can be done?

130
00:56:24.920 --> 00:56:27.880
Is there a way this electronic literature

131
00:56:27.880 --> 00:56:30.880
can be taught

132
00:56:31.280 --> 00:56:34.360
in a challenging environment?

133
00:56:34.400 --> 00:56:35.760
That’s always been my question.

134
00:56:35.760 --> 00:56:38.840
I asked that question yesterday,
and I think someone answered me.

135
00:56:38.840 --> 00:56:40.960
I think Miss Alina also answered.

136
00:56:40.960 --> 00:56:43.480
He said that
he's going to work on that project.

137
00:56:43.480 --> 00:56:44.880
So that's my challenge.

138
00:56:44.880 --> 00:56:47.680
What can we do to help this?

139
00:56:47.680 --> 00:56:50.400
That we are in a challenging environment?

140
00:56:50.400 --> 00:56:55.160
That's my own point,
where I'm going to raise the case

141
00:56:55.200 --> 00:56:58.200
and allow my mentor to talk.

142
00:56:58.240 --> 00:56:59.160
Thank you.
